/**
 * @mainpage ESP32 Environment Datalogger - Sleep Modes
 *
 * @section intro Introduction
 *
 * Periodic-wake environment datalogger implementing **Variant 2** of
 * RUS-TVZ Lab 2 ("Upravljanje potrosnjom energije mikrokontrolera" -
 * Power management of microcontrollers). The firmware demonstrates an
 * event-driven control flow where the ESP32 spends almost all of its
 * time in a low-power sleep mode and only wakes periodically to acquire
 * a temperature + humidity sample from a DHT22, buffer it, and dump the
 * last ten samples to the serial port in one block.
 *
 * @section variants Two source variants for two purposes
 *
 * The project ships **two implementations of the same firmware** because
 * the live-demo platform (Wokwi) and the target deployment platform
 * (real ESP32 silicon) impose different constraints.
 *
 * | File                              | Sleep mechanism                                                                       | Storage                       | Built by default |
 * |-----------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|------------------|
 * | `src/main.cpp`                    | Hybrid: 3 s emulated visible sleep (delay) + 1 s real `esp_deep_sleep_start()`        | `RTC_DATA_ATTR` ring buffer   | Yes (Wokwi demo) |
 * | `src/archive/main1.cpp`           | Full-duration real `esp_deep_sleep_start()` + timer wake (60 s)                       | `RTC_DATA_ATTR` ring buffer   | No  (`-e esp32-realhw`) |
 *
 * Both variants actually call the real Deep Sleep API on every cycle.
 * The Wokwi demo just trims that real Deep Sleep to ~1 second per cycle
 * (so the chip resets briefly, instead of for 60 s) and inserts a
 * visible `delay()` phase before it so the audience sees a clean
 * 3-second sleep window before the reset. The full hardware variant
 * skips the visible phase and stays in real Deep Sleep for the full
 * interval. Behaviourally both variants produce the same DHT22 +
 * 10-sample buffer + dump-and-reset flow; the buffer lives in RTC slow
 * memory in both cases, exactly as on real silicon.
 *
 * @section why_emulated Why Wokwi cannot run the real Deep Sleep path
 *
 * Two independent limitations apply:
 *
 *  1. `esp_light_sleep_start()` cannot be called repeatedly from a user
 *     task under the Arduino-ESP32 framework, because the framework
 *     ships without `CONFIG_PM_ENABLE`. The SMP-aware sleep-entry path
 *     is bypassed, the FreeRTOS idle-task spinlocks on the other core
 *     desync, and the second sleep cycle reliably trips an assertion
 *     in `spinlock_acquire()`. There is no application-level fix.
 *  2. `esp_deep_sleep_start()` *does* work in Wokwi - it resets the
 *     chip on every wake, which is correct Deep Sleep behaviour. The
 *     simulator does not model power, so even when the sleep path runs,
 *     **no actual power saving can be measured**.
 *
 * The Wokwi demo variant (`src/main.cpp`) exercises the real Deep Sleep
 * API on every cycle, but keeps the real-sleep portion short (1 s) and
 * precedes it with a visible 3-second `delay()` window. The audience
 * sees a clean sleep phase (AWAKE LED off, serial quiet), then a
 * compact `[BOOT #n via TIMER]` line on each wake instead of the full
 * banner. The full hardware variant (`src/archive/main1.cpp`) drops the
 * visible window and stays in real Deep Sleep for the entire 60-second
 * interval.
 *
 * @section spec Lab requirements vs. implementation
 *
 * | Requirement (from the spec)                              | Where it lives                                                     |
 * |----------------------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------|
 * | Sleep mode (ESP32: Deep Sleep recommended)               | `esp_deep_sleep_start()` in BOTH `src/main.cpp` and `src/archive/main1.cpp`  |
 * | Wake source: timer                                       | `esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup()` in both files                              |
 * | Simulate environmental readings                          | `DHT22` part in `diagram.json`, Adafruit DHT library in both files           |
 * | Store last 10 measurements                               | Ring buffer of size 10 in both files                                         |
 * | Use RTC memory on ESP32                                  | `RTC_DATA_ATTR` qualifiers in both files                                     |
 * | Print + reset buffer after 10 samples                    | `dumpAndReset()` in both files                                               |
 * | Separate phases: active / sleep / wake                   | `performReading()` / `emulatedSleep()` + Deep Sleep / cold-vs-timer branch   |
 * | Do not use only `delay()` for time control               | Real `esp_deep_sleep_start()` runs every cycle; `delay()` is the visible cue only |
 *
 * @section diagrams System diagrams
 *
 * @subsection sm State machine
 * @startuml
 * title Environment Datalogger - State Machine
 *
 * state "Acquisition cycle" as Cycle {
 *   state "READ"           as Read    : DHT22 readTemperature\n+ readHumidity
 *   state "BUFFERED"       as Buf     : append to ring buffer\nbufferCount++
 *   state "ACTIVITY PULSE" as Pulse   : ACTIVITY LED on\nfor 200 ms
 *   state "SLEEP"          as Sleep   : AWAKE LED off\nemulated (demo)\nor Deep Sleep (HW)
 *
 *   [*] --> Read
 *   Read --> Buf     : valid sample
 *   Read --> Pulse   : NaN twice in a row\n(skip, log [ERROR])
 *   Buf  --> Pulse
 *   Pulse --> Sleep  : bufferCount < 10
 * }
 *
 * state "DUMP" as Dump : print 10 samples\nas table\nbufferCount = 0
 * Cycle --> Dump   : bufferCount == 10
 * Dump  --> Sleep  : ready for next cycle
 * Sleep --> Read   : timer wake\n(every SLEEP_INTERVAL)
 *
 * note right of Sleep
 *   Wokwi demo (src/main.cpp):
 *     delay(3000) - emulated sleep.
 *   Real HW (src/archive/main1.cpp):
 *     esp_deep_sleep_start() with
 *     60-second timer wake.
 * end note
 * @enduml
 *
 * @subsection pf Program flow (real HW Deep Sleep variant)
 * @startuml
 * title Datalogger - Program Flow on Real Hardware
 *
 * start
 * :Power on or wake from Deep Sleep;
 * :Serial.begin(115200);
 * :bootCount++;
 * if (wake cause == TIMER?) then (yes)
 *   :uptimeS += SLEEP_INTERVAL_S;
 * else (cold boot)
 *   :uptimeS = 0;
 * endif
 * :Print banner + wake cause;
 * :Init LEDs + DHT22;
 *
 * partition "Acquisition" {
 *   :ACTIVITY LED -> HIGH;
 *   :readDht() with one retry;
 *   if (valid sample?) then (yes)
 *     :append to buffer\n[uptimeS, t, h];
 *     :totalReadings++;
 *   else (no)
 *     :log [ERROR];
 *   endif
 *   :ACTIVITY LED -> LOW;
 * }
 *
 * if (bufferCount >= 10?) then (yes)
 *   :dumpAndReset();
 * else (no)
 * endif
 *
 * :AWAKE LED -> LOW;
 * :esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup(60s);
 * :esp_deep_sleep_start();
 * note right: never returns
 *
 * stop
 * @enduml
 *
 * @subsection pmem Persistent memory layout (Deep Sleep variant)
 * @startuml
 * title RTC slow-memory layout
 *
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR Reading buffer[10]" as Buf #d6eaff
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR bufferCount  : uint8_t"  as BC  #d6eaff
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR totalReadings: uint32_t" as TR  #d6eaff
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR bootCount    : uint32_t" as BoC #d6eaff
 * rectangle "RTC_DATA_ATTR uptimeS      : uint32_t" as UpS #d6eaff
 *
 * Buf -[hidden]down-> BC
 * BC  -[hidden]down-> TR
 * TR  -[hidden]down-> BoC
 * BoC -[hidden]down-> UpS
 *
 * note right of Buf : Survives Deep Sleep\nbut NOT a hardware reset
 * note right of UpS : Accumulator that timestamps\nsamples without an RTC
 * @enduml
 *
 * @section wokwi_limits Wokwi simulator limitations
 *
 * The lab requirements explicitly require a "Wokwi-simulator
 * limitations" section in the report. The relevant ones for this
 * variant are:
 *
 *   - **No power measurement.** Wokwi does not model current draw, so
 *     none of the metrics that would justify Deep Sleep (microamps in
 *     sleep, milliamps awake, theoretical battery life) can be obtained
 *     in simulation. Real-circuit measurement (a current meter, or a
 *     tool like ARM Energy Profiler) is required.
 *   - **Light Sleep is unusable.** As described in @ref why_emulated,
 *     calling `esp_light_sleep_start()` from a user task under
 *     Arduino-ESP32 asserts on the second cycle. This is a framework
 *     limitation, not a Wokwi one - but Wokwi makes it easy to see.
 *   - **Deep Sleep works but resets the chip.** This is *correct* Deep
 *     Sleep behaviour, but it makes a live demo visually noisy because
 *     the banner reprints every interval. The Wokwi-demo variant in
 *     `src/main.cpp` sidesteps this by emulating sleep with `delay()`.
 *   - **DHT22 readings are synthetic.** Wokwi's DHT22 part returns the
 *     `temperature` / `humidity` values configured in `diagram.json`,
 *     not real environmental data. The logic of the datalogger
 *     (buffering, dumping, timestamping) is fully testable; the
 *     *content* of the readings is not realistic.
 *
 * @author Ivan Bencic
 * @date 2026
 * @version 1.0
 */
